{"causes":["Abū ʿAmra Kaysān was born into a prominent Persian family and received a traditional Islamic education.","He became involved in the politics of the Umayyad Caliphate during his youth.","The Second Muslim Civil War presented an opportunity for him to exercise his leadership skills and demonstrate his loyalty to the Umayyad dynasty."],"effects":["The conflict led to a significant increase in Shia Islam's influence in the region.","Kaysān's actions contributed to the eventual rise of Sunni-Shia sectarian tensions.","His legacy continues to shape the modern-day dynamics of the Islamic world."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Abū ʿUmarah ibn al-Ḥasan","sourceName":"Abū ʿUmarah ibn al-Ḥasan","verb":"COLLABORATES_WITH","targetSlug":"Abu Amra Kaysan","targetName":"Abū ʿAmra Kaysān","context":"Both men played key roles in the Umayyad Caliphate's efforts to quell the Second Muslim Civil War."},{"sourceSlug":"Cairo","sourceName":"Cairo","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"Medina","targetName":"Medina","context":"The conflict between the Umayyads and the Shia eventually led to a decisive battle in Medina."},{"sourceSlug":"Ali ibn Husayn","sourceName":"Ali ibn Husayn","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"Abu Amra Kaysan","targetName":"Abū ʿAmra Kaysān","context":"Kaysān was a key figure in the Shia forces that contained the Umayyad Caliphate's advances."}],"places":[{"name":"Persepolis","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Damascus","role":"Major battle site"},{"name":"Medina","role":"Decisive battle"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Abu Amra Kaysan","type":"Reference article","slug":"abu-amra-kaysan"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q12236714","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abu_Amra_Kaysan"],"tags":["Architects & Engineers","innovation-and-technology","cause-and-effect","temporal-linkage"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"","summary":"Abu Amra Kaysan (fl. late 7th century CE), also known simply as Kaysan, was an early Islamic figure associated with the origins of the Kaysaniyya — one of the first and most theologically innovative Shia Muslim movements of the formative Islamic period. A mawla (client/freedman) of Ali ibn Abi Talib or of his son Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyya, Kaysan is associated in early Islamic heresiographic sources with the circle around Mukhtar al-Thaqafi, the Kufan leader who led a major Shia revolt in 685–687 CE in the name of Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyya (Ali's son by a wife other than Fatima, hence not the son of the Prophet's daughter).

The Kaysaniyya movement that carried Kaysan's name developed distinctive theological positions that would prove highly influential on later Shia thought: belief in the imamate of Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyya, the doctrine that the imam had not died but was in occultation (ghayba) on Mount Radwa awaiting his return as the Mahdi — the first systematic development of the occultation doctrine that would become central to Twelver Shia theology in its mature form. This early Kaysanite ghayba concept anticipated by centuries the mature Twelver doctrine of the occultation of the Twelfth Imam (874 CE).

Kaysan himself is a shadowy figure whose historical reality is difficult to establish — some scholars have questioned whether he was a historical individual or a narrative vehicle through which later heresiographers organised early Shia tendencies. What is historically clear is that the theological currents associated with Kaysanism — particularly the ghayba/occultation doctrine and the exaltation of Ali's family beyond the immediate Husaynid line — contributed significantly to the diversification of early Shia thought and provided some of the conceptual vocabulary for later Ismaili and Twelver developments.","subjects":["Islamic History","Shia Islam","Medieval History","Theology","Medieval Era","Iraq","Early Islam","Religious History"],"frameworks":["RELIGIOUS_INTERPRETATION","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Abū ʿAmra Kaysān's leadership during the Second Muslim Civil War had a lasting impact on the regional dynamics of Islam.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
